.TH TALKCTL 1
.SH NAME
talkctl \- pause, resume or repace a running talk
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/talkctl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Talkctl
is a small floating window with three controls -
.BR Pause / Resume ,
.B Slower
and
.B Faster
- for a narrated talk of the kind
.IR lib/demo/*.sh
runs: say(1) narrating, session(2) driving a shell window. It has no
connection to the talk itself; it only writes a speed and a paused flag to
.BR /tmp/talk-ctl ,
which every
.B session wait
- the action that paces a talk between its typed commands - reads back,
checking a few times a second while it waits.
.PP
The same three actions are bound to keys, so a script can drive this the
same way it drives everything else:
.B p
or the space bar toggles pause,
.B s
and
.B f
step the speed down or up through
0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8,
and
.B r
resets to normal pace and unpauses.
.PP
No talk needs to be running for this to open, and no control needs to be
running for a talk to play at its ordinary pace: with
.B /tmp/talk-ctl
absent, or holding anything this has never written, a wait behaves exactly
as if this program did not exist.
.SH LIMITS
This reaches only the gaps between actions - the
.B session wait
calls a talk's pacing is built from. It does not reach
.IR say (1)
itself: narration is a separate process that blocks the talk until the
line is spoken, and there is no way to slow down or interrupt a voice
mid-utterance from outside it (the neural voice has no speed control at
all).
.B Pause
takes effect at the next wait, which in the worst case is one utterance's
length away, not immediately - a real limit on what pausing a talk can
mean here, not an oversight.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/talkctl.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR say (1),
.IR speak (1)
.SH BUGS
Speed and pause are both process-global through the one file, so two
talks running at once share one control - there is no way to steer them
independently.
